a new app that allows instagram users to  send anonymous messages is soaring in  popularity and renewing concerns about  cyber bullying and harassment that  plagued previous apps which allowed  teens to comment on one another  anonymously  the app called ngl q a was developed by  a company called deepmoji the app  invites followers to share anonymous  feedback via a link on instagram stories  that goes into the app's inbox  according to data.ai ngo which is an  abbreviation for not gonna lie has seen  five million downloads as of june 27  2022 with 2.39 million on google play  and 2.57 million on ios  ngl follows in the footsteps of websites  and apps like ask.fm whisper and yolo  that have built huge audiences by  allowing teenagers to vent privately on  social media before facing a public  backlash over child safety fears  instagram briefly blocked links to the  ngl app on june 22nd but is now active  again deep emoji says its artificial  intelligence powered content moderation  protects young users from harmful  content deepmoji did not respond for  multiple requests for comment the rise  of ngl tracks with the growth and the  collapse of earlier anonymous messaging  apps that had become popular with teens  one app called sarahah was pulled by  apple and google from their respective  app stores over claims and enabled cyber  bullying the ceo of another company  called secret voluntarily shut down the  app in 2015 after admitting his team  could not control abuse and harassment  on the platform  in saraja's case the developers banned  children from its service and promised  to step up moderation  ngl's explosive growth over the last  month has piggybacked on the popularity  of instagram with young people it also  follows snap suspending rival apps yolo  and ymk in may 2021 after being hit with  a still pending lawsuit claiming that  they broke consumer protection laws in a  case involving the suicide of an oregon  teen in june 2020. instagram itself is  currently facing criticisms for its  effects on teens and the presence of  child predators on its platform in june  meta announced it would introduce checks  using facial recognition technology to  make sure instagram users who are  allowed to join from age 13 are shown  age-appropriate content  instagram declined to comment on the ngl  app
 

